<bold>Man (n %)</bold>
</td><td align="left"/><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="3">Gender</td><td align="left">ACBs</td><td align="left">80</td><td align="left">40 (%50,0)</td><td align="left">40 (%50,0)</td><td align="left" rowspan="3">2,6</td><td align="left" rowspan="3">0,612</td></tr><tr><td align="left">ACBa</td><td align="left">80</td><td align="left">41 (%51,3)</td><td align="left">39 (%48,8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Control</td><td align="left">80</td><td align="left">42 (%52,2)</td><td align="left">38 (%47,8)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>ACBs: Adductor canal block by surgeon; ACBa: Adductor canal block by anesthesiology, SD: Standard deviation</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par29">Groups ACBa and ACBs exhibited significantly lower VAS scores compared to the control group at both 3 and 12&#x000a0;h after surgery, with group ACBa showing the lowest VAS scores among all groups (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab2" ref-type="table">2</xref>). However, at 1&#x000a0;day, 3 days, 10 days and 12 weeks after surgery, there was no significant difference in VAS scores between the ACBa and ACBs groups.</p><p id="Par51">
<table-wrap id="Tab2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Pain analyses of all groups</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left"/><th align="left">Group</th><th align="left">
<italic>n</italic>
</th><th align="left">Mean</th><th align="left">Standard Deviation</th><th align="left">X<sup>2</sup></th><th align="left">
<italic>p</italic>
